/-! # Part 3: the constants of the 1D value-plus-gradient chain -/
section Part3_ValueGradientChain

/-!
# The 1D Gaussian toy chain: scale-recursion arithmetic of value-plus-gradient composition

This part formalises the *arithmetic* of the result that, in the 1D Gaussian toy chain,
value-plus-gradient composition loses only a bounded factor: the closed forms of the constants,
their exact values, and their bounds, monotonicity and limits.

**Scope.** The 1D Gaussian toy chain; a statement about one composition method (per-scale norms
`N_k = 2^k (‖·‖₁ + β_k ‖∇·‖₁)`, composed scale by scale and summed over scales by the triangle
inequality) at Gaussian order; nothing about Yang–Mills.

**What is and is not formalised.** The constants below are defined by their closed forms, as
real-valued functions of the scale `k : ℕ`, and this part proves the inequalities, monotonicity
statements, limits and recursion bounds of these closed forms. The *identification* of the
closed forms with the optimal operator constants is the analytic part of the argument
(`τ_k = sup_z (‖R_k z‖₁ − 2‖z‖₁)/‖∇z‖₁`, `μ_k = sup_z ‖∇R_k z‖₁/‖∇z‖₁`, `γ_k = ‖R_k e_w‖₁/2`, the
minimal admissible chain `β_{k+1} = F_k(β_k)/2`, and `Θ_k ≤ 1 + 2β_k`). That part is out of
scope: no operator `R_k`, `T_k`, no norm `N_k` and no overestimate `Θ_k` appears in this part.

## The constants (closed forms)

* `ρ_k = √((4 + 2·4^{−k})/3)` (`rho`); `ρ₀ = √2`, `ρ₁ = √6/2`, strictly decreasing to `2/√3`.
* gradient constant `μ_k = 1 + 1/ρ_k` (`mu`): `μ₀ = 1 + √2/2`, strictly increasing to
  `1 + √3/2 < 2`, never attained.
* value constant `τ_k = 2/(ρ_k(2 + ρ_k))` (`tau`), and `γ_k = 1 + 2/(ρ_k(2 + ρ_k))` (`gamma`), so
  `τ_k = γ_k − 1`; `τ₀ = √2 − 1`, `γ₀ = √2`; `γ_k` strictly increasing to `(1 + 3√3)/4`.
* parameters of the kernel of `R_k`: `q_k = −(2 − ρ_k)/(2 + ρ_k)` (`qRoot`), `p_k = |q_k|`
  (`pRoot`), `a_k = 1/(2(2 + ρ_k))` (`aAmp`); identities `τ = (1 + p)/(2ρ)`,
  `μ = 1 + 4a/(1 − p)`, `γ = μ − 2a` (the case `n = 1`), and `(1 − p)/(2ρ·2a) = 1` (the
  normalisation of the threshold rule, see `betaThr`).
* the minimal chain with `ã ≡ 1`, i.e. without value loss (called the "optimal chain"; it is
  minimal only among the chains with `ã ≡ 1`), `beta`: `β₀ = 0`, `β₁ = (√2 − 1)/2`,
  `β₂ = (τ₁ + γ₁β₁)/2 = (8√6 + 16√3 + 3√2 − 27)/60`, and for `k ≥ 2`
  `β_{k+1} = (1/(2ρ_k) + (1 + 1/ρ_k) β_k)/2`.

## Main results

* `rho_gt`, `rho_strictAnti`, `rho_tendsto`: `ρ_k > 2/√3`, strictly decreasing, `ρ_k → 2/√3`.
* `mu_lt_two`, `mu_lt_sup`, `mu_strictMono`, `mu_tendsto`: `μ_k < 1 + √3/2 < 2`, strictly
  increasing, `μ_k → 1 + √3/2`.
* `gamma_zero`: `γ₀ = √2`; `gamma_strictMono`, `gamma_lt_lim`, `gamma_tendsto`.
* `beta_lt`: `β_k < 3/2 + √3` for every `k`; `beta_lt_fixedPt`: `β_k < 1/(2(ρ_k − 1))` for
  `k ≥ 2`; `beta_strictMono`; `beta_tendsto`: `β_k → 3/2 + √3`.
* `theta_bound`: `1 + 2β_k < 4 + 2√3` for every `k` (the arithmetic half of the bounded-loss
  bound `Θ_k ≤ 1 + 2β_k < 4 + 2√3`); `theta_tendsto`: `1 + 2β_k → 4 + 2√3`, so the constant cannot
  be lowered for this chain.
* `beta_one_window`, `beta_gt_threshold`: the branch inequalities at `k = 1` and for `k ≥ 2`,
  which fix the form of the recursion for `beta`.
* `gamma_prod_ge`, `gamma_prod_tendsto`, `bounded_versus_geometric`: the site-additive contrast
  `∏_{i<k} γ_i ≥ (√2)^k → ∞`.
-/

open Filter Topology

namespace ValueGradientChain

/-! ## 0. Elementary facts about `√2`, `√3`, `√6` and `(1/4)^k` -/

lemma sqrt_two_sq : Real.sqrt 2 ^ 2 = 2 := Real.sq_sqrt (by norm_num)

lemma sqrt_three_sq : Real.sqrt 3 ^ 2 = 3 := Real.sq_sqrt (by norm_num)

lemma sqrt_six_sq : Real.sqrt 6 ^ 2 = 6 := Real.sq_sqrt (by norm_num)

lemma sqrt_three_pos : 0 < Real.sqrt 3 := Real.sqrt_pos.2 (by norm_num)

lemma sqrt_two_pos : 0 < Real.sqrt 2 := Real.sqrt_pos.2 (by norm_num)

lemma one_lt_sqrt_two : 1 < Real.sqrt 2 := by nlinarith [sqrt_two_sq, sqrt_two_pos]

lemma sqrt_two_lt_three_halves : Real.sqrt 2 < 3 / 2 := by nlinarith [sqrt_two_sq, sqrt_two_pos]

lemma sqrt_two_gt : (1.4 : ℝ) < Real.sqrt 2 := (Real.lt_sqrt (by norm_num)).2 (by norm_num)

lemma sqrt_three_gt : (1.7 : ℝ) < Real.sqrt 3 := (Real.lt_sqrt (by norm_num)).2 (by norm_num)

lemma sqrt_three_lt : Real.sqrt 3 < 1.75 := (Real.sqrt_lt' (by norm_num)).2 (by norm_num)

lemma sqrt_three_lt_two : Real.sqrt 3 < 2 := by have := sqrt_three_lt; linarith

lemma one_lt_sqrt_three : 1 < Real.sqrt 3 := by have := sqrt_three_gt; linarith

lemma sqrt_six_eq : Real.sqrt 6 = Real.sqrt 2 * Real.sqrt 3 := by
  rw [← Real.sqrt_mul (by norm_num)]; norm_num

lemma quarter_pow_pos (k : ℕ) : 0 < (1 / 4 : ℝ) ^ k := by positivity

lemma quarter_pow_le_one (k : ℕ) : (1 / 4 : ℝ) ^ k ≤ 1 := pow_le_one₀ (by norm_num) (by norm_num)

lemma quarter_pow_succ_lt (k : ℕ) : (1 / 4 : ℝ) ^ (k + 1) < (1 / 4 : ℝ) ^ k := by
  rw [pow_succ]; have := quarter_pow_pos k; nlinarith

/-! ## 1. The scale parameter `ρ_k` -/

/-- The scale parameter (after the spectral reduction):
`ρ_k = √((4 + 2·4^{−k})/3) = √(2 − 4c_k)` with `c_k = (1 − 4^{−k})/6`. Here `4^{−k}` is written
`(1/4)^k`. In the 1D Gaussian toy chain this parametrises the conditional-mean map `R_k` of the
